.TH MSH 1 "" "msh" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
msh \- My Parallel SSH, an SSH fleet-management tool for Linux servers
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B msh
.I command
[flags]
.SH DESCRIPTION
msh runs commands, tasks, scripts and runbooks on one or many servers over SSH, sequentially or in parallel, directly or through a bastion, with verified host keys.
.SH COMMANDS
.SS c
.nf
Usage: msh c TASK SERVER [ARG]... [flags]

Short form of: msh task run TASK \-\-host SERVER \-\-arg ARG...
.fi
.SS completion
.nf
Usage: msh completion bash|zsh

Prints a shell completion script. Load it with:

  source <(msh completion bash)     # in ~/.bashrc
  source <(msh completion zsh)      # in ~/.zshrc, after compinit
.fi
.SS config
.nf
Usage: msh config <subcommand> [flags]

Subcommands:
  init      Write a starter configuration and inventory
  show      Show effective settings and where each value came from
  validate  Validate the configuration and the inventory

Precedence: flag > environment variable > configuration file > default.
.fi
.SS config init
.nf
Usage: msh config init [\-\-dir DIR] [\-\-force]

Writes a starter config.yaml and inventory.yaml (mode 0600) into DIR,
by default ~/.config/msh. Existing files are never overwritten unless
\-\-force is given.
.fi
.SS config show
.nf
Usage: msh config show [flags]

Shows every effective setting, its value and its source.
.fi
.SS config validate
.nf
Usage: msh config validate [flags]

Loads the configuration file and the inventory, validates both, and
prints a summary. Exits 0 when everything is valid, 3 when the
configuration or inventory is invalid, and 8 when a file cannot be read.
.fi
.SS connect
.nf
Usage: msh [flags] [user@]host[:port] [command...]
       msh [flags] \-\-host LIST [[user@]host [command...]]
       msh [flags] \-\-group NAME|\-\-tag K=V|\-\-all [command...]
       msh connect [flags] [user@]host[:port] [command...]

Connects like ssh. Without a command it opens a shell. With one it runs the
command on the server, with raw output and the remote exit status. The
server does not need to be in the inventory; if it is, its settings apply.
If it is not, ~/.ssh/config is used as ssh would. Host keys are always
verified.

\-\-host adds more servers to the destination: a,b or "a b" or @FILE. The
first word after the flags is always a destination. \-\-group, \-\-tag and \-\-all
take no destination: every word is the command. With several servers a
command runs on all of them in parallel, and without a command each server
opens in its own tmux pane.

Login uses keys from ssh\-add first, then key files, then a password: the
MSH_PASSWORD environment variable, or a prompt on the terminal.

Examples:
  msh ops@server1
  msh root@server1:2222
  msh web01 df \-h
  msh \-t web01 top
  msh \-p 2222 \-l root \-i ~/.ssh/key \-J jump@bastion.example.com server1
  msh \-\-host "web01 web02" root@db01 uptime
  msh \-\-host web01,web02 root@db01 uptime
  msh \-\-host @servers.txt root@db01 df \-h
  msh \-\-host web01,web02
  msh \-\-group web uptime
.fi
.SS exec
.nf
Usage: msh exec (\-\-host NAME | \-\-group NAME | \-\-tag K=V | \-\-all) \-\-command CMD [flags]
       msh exec \-\-local \-\-command CMD [flags]

Runs a command line on a server over SSH, like "ssh user@server CMD".
The command is interpreted by the remote user's shell. It runs on every
selected server.

With \-\-local the command runs on this machine with /bin/sh \-c instead.
.fi
.SS facts
.nf
Usage: msh facts (\-\-host NAME | \-\-group NAME | \-\-tag K=V | \-\-all) [flags]

Collects read\-only facts from servers: operating system, kernel,
architecture, CPUs, memory, root disk usage, package manager.
.fi
.SS help
.nf
Usage: msh help [command]

Without arguments, lists all commands. With a command name, shows the
usage of that command.
.fi
.SS history
.nf
Usage: msh history <subcommand> [flags]

Subcommands:
  list    List recorded runs
  show    Show one recorded run
  diff    Compare two recorded runs
  prune   Remove old runs

msh records runs in ~/.local/state/msh/history. See docs/history.md.
.fi
.SS history list
.nf
Usage: msh history list [\-\-limit N] [flags]

Lists recorded runs, newest first.
.fi
.SS history show
.nf
Usage: msh history show REF [flags]

Shows a recorded run. REF is "last", an ID, or a unique part of the start
of an ID.
.fi
.SS history diff
.nf
Usage: msh history diff REF1 REF2 [flags]

Compares two recorded runs server by server. Exits 1 if they differ.
.fi
.SS history prune
.nf
Usage: msh history prune \-\-keep N

Removes all but the newest N recorded runs.
.fi
.SS hosts
.nf
Usage: msh hosts <subcommand> [flags]

Subcommands:
  list    List servers from the inventory
  check   Check SSH connectivity to servers
.fi
.SS hosts list
.nf
Usage: msh hosts list [flags]

Lists servers from the inventory.
.fi
.SS hosts check
.nf
Usage: msh hosts check [\-\-host NAME | \-\-group NAME | \-\-tag K=V | \-\-all] [flags]

Checks SSH connectivity and authentication (like "msh ping"). Without a
selection, all servers are checked.
.fi
.SS logs
.nf
Usage: msh logs (\-\-host NAME | \-\-group NAME | \-\-tag K=V | \-\-all) \-\-file PATH [\-\-lines N] [\-\-follow] [flags]

Shows the last lines of a log file on each selected server (tail \-n N).
With \-\-follow, new lines are streamed live, prefixed with the server
name, until you press CTRL+C. Paths must be absolute and inside one of
the allowed_log_paths directories (default /var/log).
.fi
.SS packages
.nf
Usage: msh packages <subcommand> [flags]

Subcommands:
  list    List installed packages
  check   Check that packages are installed
  find    Find packages across servers
  compare Compare servers with a baseline server
  updates List pending updates

Works on servers with rpm or dpkg. msh only reads package data here.
.fi
.SS packages list
.nf
Usage: msh packages list (\-\-host NAME | \-\-group NAME | \-\-tag K=V | \-\-all) [\-\-filter PATTERN] [flags]

Lists installed packages. PATTERN is a shell\-style pattern: 'kernel*'.
.fi
.SS packages check
.nf
Usage: msh packages check (\-\-host NAME | \-\-group NAME | \-\-tag K=V | \-\-all) (\-\-package REQ[,REQ...] | \-\-file FILE) [flags]

Checks requirements on each server and exits 1 if one is not met.
A requirement is NAME, NAME>=VERSION (also =, !=, <, <=, >) or !NAME, which
means the package must not be installed. Quote requirements in the shell.
.fi
.SS packages find
.nf
Usage: msh packages find PATTERN (\-\-host NAME | \-\-group NAME | \-\-tag K=V | \-\-all) [flags]

Finds packages by name across servers and groups them by version.
PATTERN is a shell\-style pattern: 'openssl*'.
.fi
.SS packages compare
.nf
Usage: msh packages compare (\-\-group NAME | \-\-all | ...) [\-\-baseline SERVER] [flags]

Compares the installed packages of each server with a baseline server
(default: the first selected). Exits 1 when servers differ.
.fi
.SS packages updates
.nf
Usage: msh packages updates (\-\-host NAME | \-\-group NAME | \-\-tag K=V | \-\-all) [flags]

Lists pending updates (dnf, yum or apt). Nothing is installed. apt shows
what its last "apt update" found.
.fi
.SS runbook
.nf
Usage: msh runbook <subcommand> [flags]

Subcommands:
  list    List configured runbooks
  show    Show the steps of one runbook
  run     Run a runbook on servers

Runbooks are defined under "runbooks:" in the configuration file.
.fi
.SS runbook list
.nf
Usage: msh runbook list [flags]

Lists the runbooks from the configuration file.
.fi
.SS runbook show
.nf
Usage: msh runbook show NAME [flags]

Shows the steps of one runbook.
.fi
.SS runbook run
.nf
Usage: msh runbook run NAME (\-\-host NAME | \-\-group NAME | \-\-tag K=V | \-\-all) [flags]

Runs the steps of a runbook, in order, on each selected server. With
\-\-dry\-run, msh prints what would run without connecting to any server.
.fi
.SS script
.nf
Usage: msh script run (\-\-host NAME | \-\-group NAME | \-\-tag K=V | \-\-all) \-\-file PATH [\-\-interpreter NAME] [\-\-arg VALUE]... [flags]

Streams a local script to an interpreter on each selected server through
SSH standard input ("bash \-s \-\-", "sh \-s \-\-", "python3 \-", "perl \-").
The script is never copied to the server's disk. Supported interpreters:
sh, bash, python3, perl. Without \-\-interpreter the script's shebang line
decides, else sh.
.fi
.SS shell
.nf
Usage: msh shell \-\-host NAME [\-\-command CMD] [flags]
       msh shell (\-\-group NAME | \-\-all | ...) \-\-tmux [flags]

Opens an interactive login shell on one server, like "ssh user@server",
or runs CMD there with a terminal, like "ssh \-t user@server CMD".
msh exits with the remote exit status.

Example:
  msh shell \-\-host server01 \-\-command "podman exec \-it asdf /bin/bash"
.fi
.SS task
.nf
Usage: msh task <subcommand> [flags]

Subcommands:
  list    List configured tasks
  show    Show one task in detail
  run     Run a task on servers

Tasks are defined under "tasks:" in the configuration file.
.fi
.SS task list
.nf
Usage: msh task list [flags]

Lists the tasks from the configuration file.
.fi
.SS task show
.nf
Usage: msh task show NAME [flags]

Shows one task: how it executes, its arguments and its template or script.
.fi
.SS task run
.nf
Usage: msh task run NAME (\-\-host NAME | \-\-group NAME | \-\-tag K=V | \-\-all) [\-\-arg VALUE]... [\-\-param NAME=VALUE]... [flags]

Runs a configured task on the selected servers. Tasks classified as
"changes" or "dangerous" also require \-\-yes.
.fi
.SS version
.nf
Usage: msh version [\-\-short] [\-\-output text|json]

Prints the msh version, source commit, build date, Go version and platform.
.fi
.SS ping
.nf
Usage: msh ping (\-\-host NAME | \-\-group NAME | \-\-tag K=V | \-\-all) [flags]

Check SSH connectivity and authentication.
Remote command: true
.fi
.SS uname
.nf
Usage: msh uname (\-\-host NAME | \-\-group NAME | \-\-tag K=V | \-\-all) [flags]

Show system information (uname \-a).
Remote command: uname \-a
.fi
.SS kernel
.nf
Usage: msh kernel (\-\-host NAME | \-\-group NAME | \-\-tag K=V | \-\-all) [flags]

Show the kernel release (uname \-r).
Remote command: uname \-r
.fi
.SS uptime
.nf
Usage: msh uptime (\-\-host NAME | \-\-group NAME | \-\-tag K=V | \-\-all) [flags]

Show uptime and load averages.
Remote command: uptime
.fi
.SS disk
.nf
Usage: msh disk (\-\-host NAME | \-\-group NAME | \-\-tag K=V | \-\-all) [flags]

Show filesystem usage (df \-hP).
Remote command: df \-hP
.fi
.SS memory
.nf
Usage: msh memory (\-\-host NAME | \-\-group NAME | \-\-tag K=V | \-\-all) [flags]

Show memory usage in MiB (free \-m).
Remote command: free \-m
.fi
.SS services
.nf
Usage: msh services (\-\-host NAME | \-\-group NAME | \-\-tag K=V | \-\-all) [flags]

List running systemd services.
Remote command: systemctl list\-units \-\-type=service \-\-state=running \-\-no\-pager \-\-no\-legend
.fi
.SS failed\-units
.nf
Usage: msh failed\-units (\-\-host NAME | \-\-group NAME | \-\-tag K=V | \-\-all) [flags]

List failed systemd units.
Remote command: systemctl \-\-failed \-\-no\-pager \-\-no\-legend
.fi
.SS selinux
.nf
Usage: msh selinux (\-\-host NAME | \-\-group NAME | \-\-tag K=V | \-\-all) [flags]

Show the SELinux mode (getenforce).
Remote command: if command \-v getenforce >/dev/null 2>&1; then getenforce; else echo 'SELinux tools are not installed'; fi
.fi
.SH SHORTCUTS
Read-only commands with the same selection flags as exec:
.nf
Built\-in shortcuts. Each runs one read\-only remote command and accepts
the same selection, output and timeout flags as "msh exec".

  ping          Check SSH connectivity and authentication
                $ true
  uname         Show system information (uname \-a)
                $ uname \-a
  kernel        Show the kernel release (uname \-r)
                $ uname \-r
  uptime        Show uptime and load averages
                $ uptime
  disk          Show filesystem usage (df \-hP)
                $ df \-hP
  memory        Show memory usage in MiB (free \-m)
                $ free \-m
  services      List running systemd services
                $ systemctl list\-units \-\-type=service \-\-state=running \-\-no\-pager \-\-no\-legend
  failed\-units  List failed systemd units
                $ systemctl \-\-failed \-\-no\-pager \-\-no\-legend
  selinux       Show the SELinux mode (getenforce)
                $ if command \-v getenforce >/dev/null 2>&1; then getenforce; else echo 'SELinux tools are not installed'; fi
.fi
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
every selected server succeeded
.TP
.B 1
a remote command ran and exited non\-zero
.TP
.B 2
invalid command\-line input
.TP
.B 3
invalid configuration, inventory, task or task arguments
.TP
.B 4
DNS, TCP, SSH handshake, authentication, host key or bastion failure
.TP
.B 5
a connection, command or the global timeout expired
.TP
.B 6
a script could not be transferred
.TP
.B 7
tmux is unavailable or failed
.TP
.B 8
a local file could not be read or written
.TP
.B 70
an unexpected internal error (a bug in msh)
.TP
.B 130
interrupted by CTRL+C, SIGTERM or SIGHUP
